Date: Sun, 2 Aug 2026 02:51:03 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 4/5] camera: an autofocus switch, and taps that focus

Continuous autofocus is not always what is wanted: it hunts, and on a camera
whose scan takes a few seconds that is worse than deciding when to focus. Add
a switch for it, and give the taps a meaning when it is off - one tap focuses,
two focus and take the picture.

Reaching the camera's focus needs a word about how. libcamera's controls are
published by PipeWire's libcamera plugin as node properties, keyed by
SPA_PROP_START_CUSTOM plus the libcamera control id, and GStreamer's
pipewiresrc carries none of them: it exposes the stream and nothing else. So
Aperture sets them on the node itself, by running

    pw-cli set-param <node> Props { <id>: <value> }

on the node the GstDevice names in object.id. Binding the node in-process
would avoid the fork, but that means the pipewire crate, whose libspa-sys
runs bindgen and so needs a working libclang in the build - a heavy
dependency for two integers. It runs on a thread and logs rather than fails:
a camera that will not focus on demand is still a working camera.

Only integer controls arrive this way - the plugin maps bool, int32 and float
and returns early for arrays - so AfMode and AfTrigger come through while
AfWindows does not. Focusing on the point that was tapped therefore has to
wait for a change in PipeWire; focusing when the picture is tapped does not.

With autofocus on, a double tap keeps toggling the zoom, since the camera is
already focusing by itself.
